course: dictionary
lesson: 5903
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word march is a good example. Look at the words around it before you choose a meaning.
explain: «Badger trusts Mole with one more job: march the prisoners upstairs.» Walking prisoners upstairs means making them go somewhere firmly, so march means to walk them along in a firm, steady way.
explain: A capital letter and a date are strong clues. «In March» with a capital M names the month, the third month of the year. Without a capital, march is usually a verb: to walk in step, or fast and firmly. Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: March with a capital letter is the month. It is the third month of the year, with 31 days, between February and April.
fact: March without a capital is usually a verb. To march is to walk in step with others, like soldiers or a band, or to walk fast and firmly when you are cross or sure.
fact: A march is a walk by many people to show what they think, or a piece of music for marching.
fact: In the north, such as the UK, the USA and Canada, March brings spring. In Australia and New Zealand, March brings autumn.
exercise: «Badger trusts Mole with one more job: march the prisoners upstairs.» What does march mean here? | *Walk them firmly to another place | Sing them a happy song | Feed them a hot dinner | Make them run away
exercise: «In 1381 English peasants marched on London in the Peasants' Revolt.» What does marched mean here? | Ran away alone | Wrote a letter to the king | *Walked together in a big group | Slept quietly in a tent
exercise: «In a nature film a tiny ant marches across a leaf with loud crunchy footsteps.» Which meaning of march fits here? | The third month of the year | A piece of music | A quiet whisper | *To walk with steady, firm steps
test: «Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches.» What does marches mean here? | *A walk by many people to show what they think | Running in the rain | To hide quietly behind a door | The third month of the year
test: In maths, a daily amount in March is worked out with which sum, for 5 litres every day? | 5 + 31 | *5 × 31 | 31 ÷ 5 | 5 − 31
exam: Which word in «Ben saw the band march past the school in March» names the month? | past | school | *March | band
